dynotiq (0.3~beta) noble; urgency=medium

  * The background watcher is configurable: how often it looks, and whether it
    notifies about anything below a critical incident. Both are read on every
    pass, so a change takes effect without restarting the service.
  * One button puts colours, intervals and switches back to the defaults.
    History and snoozed findings stay.
  * A setting whose program is missing offers the way to install it instead of
    a dead switch.
  * Settings are grouped by subject, drop-downs have a border and an arrow, the
    version is on the page once instead of three times, and paths that sit next
    to a button moved into its tooltip.
  * Updates: packages with no installed predecessor are listed. A kernel with a
    new ABI showed the meta package alone and announced two kilobytes for a
    download of several hundred megabytes.
  * Updates: the age of the package lists is on the page and a button fetches
    them. The page only ever read what apt already knew locally.
  * Updates: a dist-upgrade that would remove packages names them and the
    command that can do it, instead of leaving the update stuck.
  * History records updates, so the app can say "since the update of 28.07 all
    CPU cores are 14 % slower" and name the order of events without claiming a
    cause. The benchmark finding in the system check says it too.
  * Benchmark page relates the latest run to the first one on this machine once
    thirty days lie between them. The existing baseline is a moving median and
    misses slow degradation.
  * A scan is only written to the history when something changed, and dyno runs
    no longer show up there as a benchmark full of zeroes.
  * The background service can remind about pending updates once a week.
    Ubuntu 26.04 turned its own notification off with no toggle in settings.
  * The dyno records on its own: the background service notices a running game,
    measures along, and has the report ready when you stop playing.
  * Frame rate and frame times in the report. They cannot be measured from
    outside, so dynotiq sets up MangoHud in its own colours and reads its
    recording, instead of building a second overlay.
  * Fix: the overview tore a gap open in a maximised window. The header is
    137 px shorter, and the live tiles at the bottom fit on screen again.
  * Fix: resetting the settings left the dyno page showing the old state of the
    same switch it carries a second time.
  * Fix: a hand-edited or half written config.json could feed a timer a string
    or a value the interface never offers. Values are checked on load.
  * Fix: one damaged line in the history file took the whole history page down.
    Lines without a usable timestamp are dropped when reading.
  * Fix: old snap revisions went unreported on a non-English desktop. snap takes
    its language from LANG and ignores LC_ALL, so the parser found nothing.
  * Fix: the journal check measured only the user journal and named the process
    rather than the unit, which for any Python service reads as "python3".
  * Fix: on an LTS, a non-LTS release is no longer offered as an upgrade.
  * Fix: the app and the background service could corrupt the state file when
    writing at the same time, which loses snoozed findings and the release
    cache. Both go through one helper with a unique temporary file and fsync.
  * Fix: a source check without network cached "unknown" for a week, and the
    finding then read like clearance.
  * Fix: clearing the user cache had no warning although it removes compiled
    game shaders, and thumbnails were counted twice in the total.
  * Fix: free space for the shader cache is checked on every partition holding
    one, not just the one with /home.
  * Fix: disabling an autostart entry with a second group wrote Hidden=true into
    that group, where nothing reads it.
  * Fix: MangoHud recordings are cleared after evaluation instead of growing by
    around 25 MB per hour played.
  * Fix: stopping a recording evaluated the run in the interface thread, and two
    clicks on rescan started two scans that undid each other.
  * Fix: the undervolting advice pointed at a curve editor nvidia-settings does
    not have on Linux.
  * Fix: the self-test wrote to the real state file and suppressed the app's own
    release check for 24 hours.
  * Fix: update-po.sh failed in a fresh clone, and the repository install page
    told people to write a .list file while the package ships a .sources one.
  * Fix: counts of one read as counts of one, and several strings that stayed
    German in an English session are translated.
  * Fix: download sizes were wrong on a multi-arch system, both architectures of
    a package overwrote each other.
  * Fix: a clock set backwards froze the release check, the source check and the
    update reminder.
  * Fix: the history file measured its limit in bytes but trimmed by lines, so
    it was rewritten in full on every append.
  * Fix: reading the NVMe temperature and switching the background service ran
    in the interface thread and froze the window when either hung.
  * Fix: stopping a recording and starting a new one immediately mixed samples
    from both runs.
  * A scan is around four seconds faster, ubuntu-drivers is asked once instead
    of twice.
  * Installed from the package, the app no longer copies icons into the home
    directory that are already in /usr/share/icons.
  * Fix: clearing old snap revisions asks for the password once instead of once
    per revision. polkit grants pkexec as auth_admin without keep, so 29
    revisions meant 29 prompts.
  * New Proton page. It reads Steam's own files for what stops a Windows game
    from starting: whether every Proton version has the container runtime it
    asks for, which title uses which version, what built a prefix, and the
    launches that already failed in Steam's compat log.
  * A missing or damaged Steam runtime is fetched again by a button, including
    the case where Steam holds the entry as installed with no depots behind it
    and answers every attempt with "Invalid platform".
  * Every prefix finding carries an action: switch the title to a Proton
    version its Windows store fits, which keeps saved games, or move the
    prefix aside where no version fits, which says what it costs first.
  * Versions Steam refuses to list, and versions unpacked into a
    compatibilitytools.d Steam does not read, are found and can be repaired.
  * Updates: packages already in the apt cache show a size, phased updates are
    listed instead of silently missing, and a failed run says why in words.
  * dynotiq is free software again, GPL-3.0-or-later for everything. The all
    rights reserved step taken in 0.2~beta1 is withdrawn.

 -- simonlinuxcraft <simonlinuxcraft@pm.me>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 12:30:00 +0200

dynotiq (0.2~beta1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Dyno records what the machine waits for: CPU, memory and disk stalls from
    the kernel pressure counters, the run queue wait of the game process and
    its paging accesses. New PAGING verdict for stutter without any heat.
  * Throttling reason now comes from the driver instead of the temperature,
    and AMD cards get a verdict at all for the first time.
  * Game check knows ntsync and Resizable BAR.
  * Steam: titles pointing at a Proton build that is not installed, and
    prefixes left behind by uninstalled games.
  * New incidents: ext4 errors, NVMe resets, uncorrectable PCIe errors, page
    flip failures, AMD ring timeouts and applications killed by systemd-oomd.
  * App check explains journal lines, and its search matches inside a name.
  * Fixes: the dyno filed runs under the runtime or the Proton build instead
    of the game, measuring threads could overlap, throttling was timed from
    the start of the recording, and AMD had no VRAM, power or throttle values.
  * Findings lead to the page that can act on them instead of opening a dialog
    that repeats the same sentence.
  * Several checks counted raw entries without asking whether the thing behind
    them still exists: Proton builds of uninstalled games, snap revisions
    regardless of size, benchmark runs regardless of age, swap without looking
    at free memory, and an HWE kernel that need not be newer than the running
    one. The pending update count now uses dist-upgrade like the updates page.

 -- simonlinuxcraft <simonlinuxcraft@pm.me>  Sat, 01 Aug 2026 21:15:00 +0200

